Rem ==================================================================
Rem mip.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: integer programming
Rem
Rem The public surface:
Rem
Rem   MipProblem(Prob As SpProblem, Ints As List Of Number)
Rem       a linear program, plus the 1-based indices of the variables
Rem       that have to come out whole
Rem   MipOptimize(mp As MipProblem) As MipSolution
Rem   MipFixed(mp As MipProblem, sol As MipSolution) As SpProblem
Rem
Rem   MipSolution: Status ("OPTIMAL" | "INFEASIBLE" |
Rem                "UNBOUNDED-OR-INFEASIBLE" | "MAXITER" | "MAXNODES"),
Rem                X, Cost, Nodes, Iterations (the node LPs' summed).
Rem
Rem Branch and bound over simplex.shoddy's SpOptimize. Solve the
Rem relaxation; if every integer variable has landed within 0.000001 of
Rem a whole number the node is a candidate answer; otherwise split the
Rem worst offender in two — x <= Floor(v) down one branch, x >= Floor(v)
Rem + 1 down the other — and solve both. A branch that cannot beat the
Rem best answer so far is not explored, which is the "bound" half and
Rem the only reason this finishes at all.
Rem
Rem Depth first, most-fractional variable, floor child first, and every
Rem node re-solved from scratch. Why that is not as wasteful as it
Rem sounds: the solver optimises the dual, so its basis is as wide as
Rem the variable count and takes no notice of how many constraints
Rem there are. A branch is one more constraint — in the dual, one more
Rem single-entry column — so a node forty deep pivots on exactly the
Rem same size of inverse as the root, and the sparse form appends that
Rem column by sharing every column already there.
Rem
Rem No shadow prices here, and that is deliberate. The duals of the
Rem last node's LP are the sensitivities of that node, not of the
Rem integer problem, and an integer problem's true sensitivities are
Rem not linear-programming duals at all. MipFixed is the honest way to
Rem the useful half: it pins every integer variable at the answer's
Rem value and hands back the LP that remains, whose duals and reduced
Rem costs are exactly the sensitivity of the continuous variables given
Rem the decisions taken.
Rem
Rem Ceiling: the LP under it is a hundreds-of-variables solver, and
Rem branch and bound multiplies that by however many nodes the tree
Rem takes. Pure throughout; effects stay with the caller.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "sparse.shoddy"
Include "simplex.shoddy"

Type MipProblem
    Prob As SpProblem
    Ints As List Of Number

Type MipSolution
    Status     As String
    X          As Array Of Number
    Cost       As Number
    Nodes      As Number
    Iterations As Number

Rem The walk's running state (internal plumbing). Found says whether
Rem BestX and BestC mean anything yet; Halt is empty while the search
Rem may continue and carries the status that stopped it if it could
Rem not.

Type MipState
    Found As Boolean
    BestX As Array Of Number
    BestC As Number
    Nodes As Number
    Iters As Number
    Halt  As String

Rem ---- branching rows ---------------------------------------------------
Rem A bound on one variable is a one-entry row: the sparse form appends
Rem it by touching that single column and sharing the rest.

Rem (The bound's own right-hand side is named `at` rather than `rhs`:
Rem words are case-insensitive here, so a parameter spelled rhs would
Rem take the Rhs accessor's place inside this Def and quietly break it.)

Def MipBound(p As SpProblem, k As Number, coef As Number, at As Number) As SpProblem
    SpProblem(Costs(p), SpAddRow(SA(p), Dim(1, k), Dim(1, coef)), Concat(Rhs(p), Dim(1, at)))

Rem ---- choosing what to split on ---------------------------------------

Def MipFracOf(v As Number) As Number
    Min(v - Floor(v), Floor(v) + 1 - v)

Rem The integer variable standing furthest from a whole number, 0 when
Rem they are all within tolerance of one. Strict improvement, so a tie
Rem stays with the one that came first in Ints - which MipOptimize
Rem sorts, making that the lowest index.

Def MipWorstAt(xs As Array Of Number, ints As List Of Number, i As Number, bestK As Number, bestF As Number) As Number
    If i > Length(ints) Then
        bestK
    Else
        Let f = MipFracOf(Nth(xs, Nth(ints, i)))
        If f > 0.000001 And f > bestF Then
            MipWorstAt(xs, ints, i + 1, Nth(ints, i), f)
        Else
            MipWorstAt(xs, ints, i + 1, bestK, bestF)

Def MipWorst(xs As Array Of Number, ints As List Of Number) As Number
    MipWorstAt(xs, ints, 1, 0, 0)

Rem ---- the walk ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem One self-recursive word, threading the incumbent and the counts
Rem through. The first child is called for its answer and the second in
Rem tail position, so the live depth is the depth of the tree and not
Rem the number of nodes in it.
Rem
Rem Below the root, a relaxation that comes back UNBOUNDED-OR-INFEASIBLE
Rem is pruned rather than passed on: a child's feasible region sits
Rem inside its parent's, and the parent had an optimum, so the child
Rem cannot be unbounded - it is empty, and empty is a prune. At the
Rem root there is no such parent, and the status is the honest answer.

Def MipSearch(p As SpProblem, ints As List Of Number, depth As Number, st As MipState) As MipState
    If Halt(st) <> "" Then
        st
    Else
        If Nodes(st) >= 100000 Then
            With(st, Halt = "MAXNODES")
        Else
            Let sol = SpOptimize(p)
            Let st1 = With(st, Nodes = Nodes(st) + 1, Iters = Iters(st) + Iterations(sol))
            Select Case Status(sol)
                Case "OPTIMAL"
                    If Found(st1) And Cost(sol) >= BestC(st1) - 0.000001 Then
                        Rem this branch cannot beat the answer in hand
                        st1
                    Else
                        Let k = MipWorst(X(sol), ints)
                        If k = 0 Then
                            With(st1, Found = True, BestX = X(sol), BestC = Cost(sol))
                        Else
                            Let f = Floor(Nth(X(sol), k))
                            Let below = MipSearch(MipBound(p, k, 0 - 1, 0 - f), ints, depth + 1, st1)
                            MipSearch(MipBound(p, k, 1, f + 1), ints, depth + 1, below)
                Case "MAXITER"
                    With(st1, Halt = "MAXITER")
                Case "INFEASIBLE"
                    st1
                Case Else
                    If depth = 0 Then
                        With(st1, Halt = Status(sol))
                    Else
                        st1

Def MipAnswer(st As MipState) As MipSolution
    If Halt(st) <> "" Then
        MipSolution(Halt(st), Dim(0, 0), 0, Nodes(st), Iters(st))
    Else
        If Found(st) Then
            MipSolution("OPTIMAL", BestX(st), BestC(st), Nodes(st), Iters(st))
        Else
            Rem the tree was walked out and nothing whole was found
            MipSolution("INFEASIBLE", Dim(0, 0), 0, Nodes(st), Iters(st))

Def MipOptimize(mp As MipProblem) As MipSolution
    If Any(Ints(mp), Fn(k) => k < 1 Or k > Length(Costs(Prob(mp)))) Then
        Error("MIP: INTEGER VARIABLE INDEX OUT OF RANGE")
    Else
        MipAnswer(MipSearch(Prob(mp), Sort(Ints(mp)), 0,
                            MipState(False, Dim(0, 0), 0, 0, 0, "")))

Rem ---- sensitivity, after the decisions are taken -----------------------
Rem Every integer variable pinned at the value the answer gave it, as
Rem the pair of one-entry rows an MPS FX bound would have been. Solve
Rem what comes back and its shadow prices and reduced costs are the
Rem sensitivity of the continuous variables given those decisions,
Rem which is a question with a real answer - unlike the sensitivity of
Rem the integer problem itself, which no LP dual reports.

Def MipPin(p As SpProblem, k As Number, v As Number) As SpProblem
    MipBound(MipBound(p, k, 1, v), k, 0 - 1, 0 - v)

Def MipFixed(mp As MipProblem, sol As MipSolution) As SpProblem
    If Status(sol) <> "OPTIMAL" Then
        Error("MIPFIXED: THERE IS NO OPTIMUM TO PIN THE INTEGERS AT")
    Else
        Fold(Ints(mp), Prob(mp), Fn(p, k) => MipPin(p, k, Round(Nth(X(sol), k))))
